## Blocking Finding

**[P2] The same-baseline PASS does not exercise observable supported retention.**
Location: `.tmp/resume182-4-layout315/probe-affected1.py:39-56`, with the
support predicate at `layout-candidate.py:639-648`.

Static reasoning from the saved fixture, not a new execution:

- The full glyphs are 40 pixels high, establishing `glyph_h=40`. The connected
  sloped component spans rows `[100,116)` and columns `[250,383)`: height16,
  width133, area266, density0.125. It satisfies the new predicate at its exact
  inclusive upper-height boundary.
- The added same-baseline glyph starts at x450. Its horizontal separation from
  the component's exclusive stop383 is **67**, exceeding the allowed
  `1.5 * glyph_h = 60`. The glyph at x490 is still farther away. Neither can
  supply the required support, despite sharing the component's centre y108.
- The upper glyphs occupy rows `[40,80)`, centre60. Their centre difference48
  exceeds the same-baseline tolerance30 (the code compares doubled centres
  against60). Their vertical proximity is precisely the borrowing the new
  class disallows. The other body glyphs are too distant vertically.
- The added glyphs keep the projection live across rows88..127; this is a
  full-height row, not an eligible short-row exception. Consequently the new
  first component-filter loop removes the sparse component in this case.
  Nevertheless, both saved final results are `[[40,127],[180,219],[340,379]]`.
  Surviving full glyphs already cover the component's y extent, so final band
  equality conceals the counting-copy component removal.

Concrete risk: a regression that also rejects genuinely supported sparse
components could still pass all three present contrasts. The only purported
positive support control supplies no in-range horizontal support and cannot
observe retention. This is an evidence defect, not a claim that the current
predicate fails when a qualifying same-baseline glyph is actually present.

Required revision scope: provide a positive support contrast whose glyph
actually satisfies both geometric support conditions and whose assertion can
distinguish retained versus removed counting-copy ink, or an equivalent
component-sensitive decision trace. Keep the existing receipts and assertions
as historical facts. No code change is demanded by this finding. This review
does not authorize executing that contrast or rerunning any stage.

